 "authority": "PRIMARY. Photograph of the punched 2nd Ed counter set, printed on p.4 of the scanned 1971 folio. Every strength/movement pair below was read BY EYE off a zoomed, rotation-corrected crop of that photograph and each row names the crop that proves it. No VASSAL module art, no org.pdf, no rules.pdf, no Sabin document contributed anything to this file (see RULEBOOK_VERIFIED.md section 1).",
 "source": {
  "file": "C:/VassalLibrary/launchbox/Manuals/VASSAL/Napoleon at Waterloo (2nd Ed)/NapoleonatWaterloo.pdf",
  "page": 4,
  "embedded_image": "single JPEG, extracted at native resolution, 4400x3400 px",
  "render": "naw_render.page_image(doc, 4, 400) -> C:/VassalNaW/pages/ed2_p4_native.png",
  "counter_size_px": "approx 220x216 in the native scan",
  "orientation": "every counter is printed 90 degrees off upright in the photograph; crops are rotated -90 (clockwise) to read (evidence a_00_overview.png vs any a_cell_*.png)"
 },
 "method": {
  "geometry": "four rectangular blocks located by a saturation mask column/row projection, then cut on a regular per-block grid; the grid, the two empty Allied cells, the one empty Prussian cell and the one detached French counter are all declared in naw_counters.py BLOCKS/SKIP and are reproducible with `python naw_counters.py sheet`",
  "adjudication": "BY EYE, off a_sheet_<block>.png (3x) and, for every contested cell, a_cell_<block>_rNcN.png (4x-6x). Whole-block rotated views a_02_block_<block>_raw.png were used to confirm the counter count and that no counter is concealed beneath another.",
  "ocr": "pytesseract / Tesseract 5 was run as a cross-check only and is NOT authoritative. `python naw_counters.py ocr` returns a non-empty string for 51 of 53 unit counters; it agrees exactly with the by-eye reading on 40, returns a truncated or over-long fragment on 9, is empty on 2, and CONTRADICTS the by-eye reading on 2 (french r1c5 read '1-4' where the counter plainly prints 7-4, french r5c4 read '3-4' where it plainly prints 5-4). Both contradictions were re-cut at 6x and resolved by eye against the printed digit. A first OCR pass over the whole counter face returned 3 non-empty strings out of 54 and is recorded here only as the reason the number region is cropped before recognition.",
  "unit_type_discrimination": "by the NATO-style symbol inside the rectangle, read on the zoomed crops: crossed diagonals = infantry, a single diagonal = cavalry, a filled dot = artillery. The three are unambiguous at 4x on every counter in the set. Corroborated by movement allowance (every artillery counter prints a 3) and by the folio's own Playing Pieces case, which names exactly these three types."
 },
